From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 00:03:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: apple: Add the T8010 PDM microphone controller

The built-in microphones of the iPad 6 and iPad 7 are PDM microphones
recorded by the A10's PDM controller. Each microphone has a HiFi
decimator that filters its bit stream down to 16-bit PCM; the controller
hands the samples to SmartIO DMA (receive endpoint 0x5b), and the PDM
clock comes from the Always-On Processor's audio service.

Add a CPU DAI for it, following iPadOS 17.7.11's AppleT8010PDMAudio:

- hw_params starts the PDM clock at the filter's PDM rate, ungates the
  decimators and waits the microphones' 50 ms startup delay;
- prepare flushes and resets the controller, enables one decimator per
  channel, programs the decimators (ratios, filter lengths, the FIR
  coefficients, the per-decimator timing words) and the FIFO watermark;
- trigger runs the controller after the DMA start and stops it before
  the DMA stops, as iOS orders it;
- the IRQ reports FIFO overflows and underflows.

The controller's sample shift is a gain: iOS sets 16 for 16-bit samples,
and each step below adds 6 dB, also while the stream runs (on J71b, 11
raised the room noise from -74 to -44 dBFS). Expose it as "Mic Capture
Volume", 0 to +42 dB, default +18 dB: speech then peaks near -14 dBFS
with headroom left for the microphones' DC offset, which the gain scales
as well.

The 48 kHz filter is the one of the "dmic" node in the J71b, J72b and
J171 Apple device trees, identical on all three. Other rates are left
out until they are needed.

Assisted-by: LLM
